 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A single P-channel MOSFET between VIN and VOUT, run as an ideal diode from 1.5 V to 5.5 V and up to 1.5 A continuous. A comparator weighs CE against VIN: CE more than 80 mV above VIN turns the MOSFET off, CE more than 250 mV below VIN turns it on (VOFF and VON in the electrical table). Logic between the comparator and the gate also drives the ST output."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse polarity protection",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With a negative voltage on VIN the device remains off and blocks current to the load; VIN withstands –6 V. In a single always-on device, CE may be grounded so it never goes negative relative to GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse current blocking",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With CE on VOUT, the switch opens when VOUT rises above VIN by VOFF, so reverse current (IRCB) is stopped in tOFF, and closes again when VOUT falls below VIN by VON. While off, current can still pass forward through the body diode at VFWD."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Status output",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "ST is open drain. It is pulled low while the chip is disabled and released (high impedance) while it is enabled, so a pull-up gives high for ON and low for OFF. tST, from CE going high to ST responding, is 1 µs typical. ST sinks 1 mA at 0.1 V max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ORing two supplies",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Two devices with outputs joined and each CE wired to the other's input pass the higher supply, make-before-break, with no current between the supplies. If the two inputs stay equal both turn off; using the priority device's ST, with a pull-up, as the other device's CE makes both switch at the same moment and avoids this. Where the inputs can be reversed, a series resistor in each CE line limits CE current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input supply",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "VIN must be well regulated, sit near the part and be able to supply load steps. An input capacitor CIN of 1 µF is usually enough to stop the input dipping when the switch closes; a slow supply or large load steps can need more bulk capacitance at the input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Keep every trace short and put CIN and the output capacitor right at the pins, limiting trace inductance. VIN, VOUT and GND traces should be wide."
  }
 ],
